.Dd November 11, 2026
.Dt WATCHDOG 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm watchdog
.Nd Intel TCO hardware watchdog
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa /dev/watchdog
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The version 2 TCO timer of an Intel ICH9 or Lynx Point-LP resets the
machine when an armed timer is left unfed through both expiry stages.
The kernel identifies the chipset before accessing PMBASE plus 0x60
and the NO_REBOOT bit of RCBA plus 0x3410.
Other chipsets have no device node.
The timer is halted at boot, unless the firmware left it counting toward
a reset or the kernel's command line has
.Cm rach2.itco.arm ,
and it is halted at an orderly shutdown.
.Pp
A timer running at boot is fed by the kernel every half timeout, from the
boot processor's tick, until a program opens the device, as Linux's
watchdog core feeds a watchdog that runs before user space takes it over.
The tick stops with the kernel, at a panic or when the boot processor
hangs with its interrupts off, and the chipset then resets the machine
after both stages of the timer, twice the timeout after the last feed.
.Cm rach2.itco.arm
starts the timer at boot for that purpose, as firmware that leaves it
running would.
These options of Linux's are read from the command line:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm iTCO_wdt.heartbeat Ns = Ns Ar SECONDS
The timeout at boot, 3 through 614 seconds; 30 when not given or out of
range.
.It Cm iTCO_wdt.nowayout Ns = Ns Ar BOOL
With a true value, the timer cannot be stopped once a program has
started it: a magic close and
.Dv WDIOS_DISABLECARD
fail with
.Er EBUSY
and leave it running, as on Linux.
The kernel feeds it no more, so a program that stops feeding it lets the
machine reset.
.It Cm iTCO_wdt.turn_SMI_watchdog_clear_off Ns = Ns Ar N
With
.Ar N
2 or more, clear TCO_EN in SMI_EN at PMBASE plus 0x30 at boot, so that
the first stage's expiry raises no SMI to the firmware and the second
stage resets the machine through the chipset alone.
The default, 1, leaves TCO_EN as the firmware set it, as Linux does on
version 2; on the Lenovo the firmware's SMI handler then leaves the
machine down at the first expiry, not reset.
.It Cm watchdog.handle_boot_enabled Ns = Ns Ar BOOL
With a false value, the kernel does not feed a timer running at boot.
.It Cm watchdog.open_timeout Ns = Ns Ar SECONDS
The kernel feeds a timer running at boot only until this many seconds
after the device is registered, the last feed a timeout before the end,
unless a program opens the device first; 0, the default, for no limit.
.El
.Pp
Opening the device arms the timer.
Only one open file description may hold it; a second open fails with
.Er EBUSY .
A nonempty write feeds it while it runs.
Closing after a write containing the byte
.Ql V
halts it, unless a later nonempty write lacked that byte.
A zero-length write changes nothing.
A close without that magic byte feeds once and leaves the timer armed,
including a close caused by a process exit or signal.
A timer halted by
.Dv WDIOC_SETOPTIONS
stays halted at close.
The timer may be opened again.
Once a program has opened the device, the kernel no longer feeds the
timer; it stays with the program until a magic close or
.Dv WDIOS_DISABLECARD
halts it.
.Pp
The interface uses Linux's ioctl numbers and structures from
.In linux/watchdog.h :
.Bl -tag -width WDIOC_GETBOOTSTATUS
.It Dv WDIOC_GETSUPPORT
Return a 40-byte
.Vt watchdog_info
with identity
.Ql iTCO_wdt ,
firmware version 2, and SETTIMEOUT, KEEPALIVEPING and MAGICCLOSE options.
.It Dv WDIOC_GETSTATUS
Return Linux's status flags for a driver without its own:
WDIOF_MAGICCLOSE (0x100) while the last nonempty write held the magic byte,
and WDIOF_KEEPALIVEPING (0x8000) if the timer was armed or fed since the
previous call, which clears that flag.
.It Dv WDIOC_GETBOOTSTATUS
Return an integer zero, as Linux's iTCO driver does.
.It Dv WDIOC_KEEPALIVE
Feed the timer while it runs; the argument is unused.
.It Dv WDIOC_SETTIMEOUT
Read an integer timeout, 3 through 614 seconds, program it, and feed the
timer if it runs.
Write the requested seconds back through the same pointer.
The default is 30 seconds.
The hardware counts 0.6-second ticks; seconds are converted by multiplying
by 10 and dividing by 6, with truncation, as Linux's version 2 driver does.
.It Dv WDIOC_GETTIMEOUT
Return the requested timeout in seconds.
.It Dv WDIOC_GETTIMELEFT
Return the current countdown in whole seconds, truncated.
This reads the current stage, as Linux does.
.It Dv WDIOC_SETOPTIONS
Read an integer mask: WDIOS_DISABLECARD (1) halts the timer;
WDIOS_ENABLECARD (2) arms it.
When both are set, halt precedes arm.
Arming a running timer or halting a halted one changes nothing.
A mask containing neither fails with
.Er ENOTTY ,
as on Linux.
.El
.Pp
A read is unsupported.
Unknown ioctls fail with
.Er ENOTTY .
Bad user pointers fail with
.Er EFAULT ;
an invalid timeout, or a timer register that refuses it, with
.Er EINVAL ;
a halt or NO_REBOOT bit that refuses a change with
.Er EIO ;
and a stop under nowayout with
.Er EBUSY .
There are no unbounded waits in the driver.
.Sh FILES
.Pa /dev/watchdog
is a character device, major 10, minor 130, mode 0600, owned by root.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ioctl 2 ,
.Xr open 2 ,
.Xr write 2 ,
.Xr acpi 4 ,
.Xr pstore 5
